# CODEOWNERS — subsystem ownership map (GROWTH TEMPLATE)
#
# Plume is a solo project TODAY: every path maps to the single maintainer below. This file is
# structured per-subsystem anyway so that, as the team grows, ownership can be reassigned box
# by box WITHOUT restructuring — add the new handles on the relevant lines (e.g. @alice, or a
# team as @org/team-name).
#
# @guatxlabs IS A REAL, WORKING OWNER — do not "fix" it. It is the GitHub account that owns this
# repository (account type: User), so GitHub resolves it and assigns it. This is worth stating
# because the reverse case is a classic silent failure: an ORGANISATION name on its own is NOT a
# valid code owner. GitHub accepts only @user, @org/team-name, or the e-mail address of an
# account — an org handle alone renders as "Unknown owner" and assigns NOBODY, so the file looks
# like a guard while doing nothing. If ownership ever moves to an organisation, these lines must
# become @org/team-name, never @org.
#
# Two further conditions, both outside this file — it is not self-sufficient:
#   · every owner listed here needs EXPLICIT write access to the repository;
#   · assignment is advisory until branch protection enables "Require review from Code Owners".
#
# Ownership boundaries mirror docs/MODULE-MAP.md. "Guarded" subsystems (masking, RBAC, cold
# crypto, boot) should keep the maintainer (or a security-owner handle) as a REQUIRED reviewer
# even after delegation — see the "safely ownable independently?" column in the module map.
#
# Syntax: <path glob>  <owner...>   Later matches win. Keep the catch-all first.
#
# TODO(team-growth): delegate subsystems to per-person/per-team handles as contributors join, and
# add a security-owner alongside the maintainer on the guarded paths.

